@@ -0,0 +1,123 @@
+//! End of flight: from the takeover to the sea, under explicitly weighted hypotheses about how
+//! the flight ended and families of what happened in the cockpit, piloted and unpiloted alike.
+//!
+//! How it enters the estimate. The runner hands over each posterior trajectory when the module
+//! takes over (terminal hook, being built on core/stages): at a flame-out, or at the start of a
+//! powered descent. This module flies descents to the sea and returns impact samples with their
+//! energy, the states at the 00:19 epochs after the takeover for the runner to score, and one
+//! observation term of its own: the 00:19:29.416 log-on, scored under each hypothesis.
+//!
+//! Hypotheses (`endings`; Pete, 28 Sep 2026: exhaustion causing the log-on is not an axiom):
+//! exhausted before the log-on, exhausted after it (powered through 00:19), and a powered
+//! impact before exhaustion. Until the core models fuel, the later two are bounded by a
+//! PLACEHOLDER window ending at 01:16:05 (Boeing Table 4's longest endurance, 6.8 h from arc 1);
+//! with fuel, each path's exhaustion time decides between the first two. Equal priors; results
+//! are reported per hypothesis, then per family. ASSUMPTION: until its takeover, a path flies on
+//! the core's dynamics, so under the later two endings the flight after 00:19 follows the core's
+//! autopilot-mode cruise until the flame-out or the powered descent.
+//!
+//! Model (details and sources in each file):
+//! - `aero`: Poll-Schumann B777-200 point-mass polar (pycontrails, Apache-2.0) and its
+//!   maximum-continuous thrust, plus uncertain post-flame-out increments: windmilling
+//!   engines and ram-air turbine, drag rise above the usable lift coefficient, and wave-drag
+//!   growth beyond Mach 0.89, where every public model and the NASA CRM data (M <= 0.87)
+//!   end. Flight beyond it is labelled extrapolated.
+//! - `flight`: 3-DOF point mass over WGS-84 in the runner's wind and temperature, with
+//!   control laws for lift and bank.
+//! - `descent`: the autopilot's single-engine phase from the first flame-out, then a family's
+//!   flight from the second.
+//! - `families`: after a flame-out, normal-law and stick-fixed unpiloted descents (the residual
+//!   asymmetry an input; control lost at the first or the second flame-out) and piloted glides,
+//!   ditchings and dives; before exhaustion, a powered upset and a piloted powered dive,
+//!   descent or ditching. Each on its own unit cube of priors, piloted and unpiloted halves.
+//! - `timing`: flame-out timing and the APU/SDU log-on lag. The takeover draws the first
+//!   flame-out; each descent draws the second given it, as one of its family's coordinates.
+//! - `sampler`, `proposal`: per trajectory, pilot rounds scored with the runner's callback (and
+//!   this module's log-on term) adapt a defensive mixture on each family's unit cube (half plain
+//!   prior, half boxes about the weighted pilots, boxes only in the coordinates the pilots
+//!   constrain); the final draws come fresh from it with exact ln(prior/proposal). Generic in
+//!   what is simulated: nothing assumes the aircraft is unpowered after the takeover.
+//!
+//! The log-on (observation `m0019a.logon`: the 00:19:29.416 request, its 00:19:37.443
+//! acknowledgement, and the aircraft still flying at the acknowledgement) is scored under each
+//! ending on one absolute scale, density per second of log-on time (`timing::Logon`):
+//! - exhausted before the log-on: the lag from the second flame-out (APU about 60 s, SDU about
+//!   60 s; gamma, mean 120 s, sd 20 s by default);
+//! - the other two: other-cause log-ons at a declared rate, the first after the 00:10:59
+//!   hand-off coming at the observed time. The rate is swept (0.1, 0.3, 1 per hour; this
+//!   flight had one such log-on, at 18:25, in about seven hours) and the endings' evidence is
+//!   reported against it. Under all three, no other-cause log-on came before 00:19:29.416.
+//!
+//! Until the terminal hook reaches main, `preview` (ignored test) flies every family from
+//! hand-offs taken from the base run's routes (prepare/handoffs.py) in still air, and
+//! prepare/preview.py draws per-family results. It is a physics preview and not evidence:
+//! the real numbers come from the ERA5 descent atmosphere through the terminal hook.
+//!
+//! Departures from published work, and why:
+//! - Boeing's ten engineering-simulator runs (ATSB 2016) are calibration data for this open
+//!   model, not a prior over what happened: they assume nobody flew the aircraft.
+//! - Pitch after the second flame-out is an uncertain alternative. In the six long Boeing
+//!   runs the phugoid period is 81-86 s at about 250 kt, where a stick-fixed (constant-CL)
+//!   aircraft would show 58 s; a load-factor law with speed stability (777 normal mode,
+//!   C*U-like) reproduces it. The four short runs, which diverge to 50-60 deg of bank and
+//!   Mach 0.97-1.04, look stick-fixed. The brief's "secondary mode" is therefore one option,
+//!   not the assumption (prepare/boeing_runs.py).
+//! - Windmilling engines and ram-air turbine add drag coefficient 0.0025-0.0055 to the clean
+//!   Poll-Schumann polar (about +15-30% at best glide). Boeing's driftdown figure, "0.0034 nm
+//!   per foot of altitude", 120 NM from FL350 (SIR App. 1.6E), read as a steady best glide
+//!   from FL350, needs 0.004 (model: 118.5 NM at 215 KEAS); the long simulator spirals, L/D
+//!   17.5-18.2, need 0.0045. Read instead as a bare L/D of 20.7 it would need almost none;
+//!   the steady-glide reading is how performance data are tabulated (`calibrate::driftdown`).
+//!
+//! Calibration against the six long Boeing runs (prepare/boeing_runs.py, `calibrate`), flown
+//! from each run's second flame-out in the simulator's fitted wind (from 246 deg, 23.5 kt at
+//! sea level rising to 43 kt at 25,000 ft). Normal-mode law: speed stiffness 0.8 about a
+//! reference that moves from the disconnect speed to a 195 KEAS floor with a 60 s time
+//! constant, sensed through a 0.8-0.85 s lag; drag 0.0045 above the clean polar; residual
+//! asymmetry of 0.7 deg/s left, spiral mode -1/60 s. Result, model against runs: 1,330-1,390 s
+//! against 1,361-1,458 s from the loss of control to the sea, 23-24 NM against 16-34 NM,
+//! phugoid 85-86 s against 81.5-86 s, peak descent 6,000-7,000 fpm against 4,560-7,140 fpm,
+//! peak 250-262 KEAS against 233-264. The phugoid's growth is very sensitive to the lag
+//! (linear theory: it turns unstable above about 0.5 s), so the lag is an uncertain input.
+//! Not reproduced: the step changes of bank in runs 05, 08 and 09 and run 05's final dive.
+//!
+//! The four short runs (control lost at the first flame-out; 4-7 min and 9-20 NM to the sea;
+//! 33,000-58,000 fpm; 570-616 KEAS at Mach 0.97-1.04) need a stick-fixed law with a strong
+//! Mach tuck (trimmed lift falling 1-3.5% per 0.01 Mach above 0.75), a trim offset of up to
+//! 10% below one g, a divergent spiral and less transonic drag than Poll-Schumann
+//! extrapolates. No single setting reproduces all four: the model dives more directly than
+//! Boeing's first-dive-then-zoom sequences. ATSB warned that these runs left the simulator's
+//! own database, so they bound the stick-fixed family's priors rather than fix a point.
+
+#![allow(dead_code)] // The terminal hook (core/stages) will call into these modules.
+
+mod aero;
+mod descent;
+mod endings;
+mod families;
+mod flight;
+mod proposal;
+mod sampler;
+mod sampling;
+mod timing;
+
+#[cfg(test)]
+mod calibrate;
+#[cfg(test)]
+mod preview;
+
+use hypothesis::Hypothesis;
+use serde::Deserialize;
+
+#[derive(Deserialize)]
+#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]
+struct Params {}
+
+struct EndOfFlight;
+
+pub fn new(params: &toml::Value) -> Result<Box<dyn Hypothesis>, String> {
+    let _: Params = params.clone().try_into().map_err(|e| format!("end-of-flight: {e}"))?;
+    Ok(Box::new(EndOfFlight))
+}
+
+impl Hypothesis for EndOfFlight {}
